U01: Furthermore, son of noble family, the Tathāgata’s aspiration is unfailing. What is that aspiration? It is this: aspiration for virtuous qualities. And what is that? The Tathāgata’s aspiration for great love is unfailing. The Tathāgata’s aspiration for great compassion is unfailing. The aspiration for teaching the Dharma is unfailing. The aspiration for taming sentient beings is unfailing. The aspiration for bringing sentient beings to full maturity is unfailing. The aspiration for complete seclusion is unfailing. The aspiration for causing sentient beings to truly take hold of awakening is unfailing. The aspiration for not interrupting the lineage of the Three Jewels is unfailing.
U02: The Tathāgata does not proceed by way of aspiration; rather, the aspiration of the Tathāgata is preceded by primordial wisdom. Therefore, it is said that the aspiration of the Tathāgata does not decline. He also teaches the Dharma in order to bring the unsurpassed aspiration to perfection for all sentient beings. This is the twenty-first of the Tathāgata’s enlightened activities. Regarding that, it is said as follows:
U03: The Victor’s aspiration is a constant desire for and delight in the way of virtuous qualities. In order to bring sentient beings to complete liberation, he also bestows the teachings of love and compassion. He never loses his aspiration to mature sentient beings and his delight in forest retreats. He leads vast numbers to embrace awakening and ensures the lineage of the Three Jewels is never broken.
U04: One does not go astray through desire, hatred, fear, or delusion. Having become distinguished, the wise one, through knowledge, comes to realize all phenomena that are constructed. Seeing beings who are devoid of aspiration and are lazy, the Victors engage them in aspiration. Through that which is peerless knowledge, one discerns the actions of beings that are based on aspiration.
U05: Furthermore, O son of good family, the Tathāgata’s diligence does not diminish. The Tathāgata’s diligence is as follows: it is the diligence of not forsaking sentient beings who are to be disciplined, and the diligence of not disparaging those who listen to the Dharma. If the Tathāgata finds a listener who is a vessel for the Dharma and who would not grow weary of hearing the Dharma even for an eon, the Tathāgata will teach the Dharma; even if his physical constitution were deprived of food, he would not rise for an eon. Out of regard for sentient beings, the Tathāgata would travel across as many buddhafields as there are grains of sand in the river Gaṅgā for the sake of even a single being to be disciplined by a Buddha; yet the Tathāgata would never experience physical fatigue, vocal exhaustion, or mental weariness.
U06: The Tathāgata is one whose mind is thoroughly disciplined, who has initiated diligence, and who speaks in praise of diligence; he commends to sentient beings that diligence of right application through which sentient beings will attain noble liberation. This is the twenty-second activity of the Tathāgata. Regarding that, it is said as follows:
U07: Through whatever diligence the lion among men becomes noble, one always speaks in praise of that power of diligence; the power of diligence does not decline in any way, and having found a suitable vessel, it also teaches the Dharma.
U08: The diligence of the Sugatas is not something that remains static; in body, speech, and mind, there is no weariness anywhere. Just as the self-arisen diligence is entirely without fault, in that same way, one causes sentient beings whose minds have been trained to correctly undertake [the path].
U09: Furthermore, O son of good family, the Tathāgata’s mindfulness is never impaired in any way, in any aspect, or in any respect. How so? The Tathāgata is without forgetfulness. O son of good family, from the moment the Tathāgata fully awakened to unsurpassed, perfect, complete enlightenment, even though he perceives the mental continuums of sentient beings of the past, future, and present, the Tathāgata is entirely without forgetfulness regarding them. Even though he knows the conduct of sentient beings exactly as it is, the Tathāgata does not have to exert his wisdom for that purpose. Because the Tathāgata establishes the three aggregates, engages with the faculties of sentient beings, engages with the intentions of sentient beings, and perceives the conduct of sentient beings, his mindfulness is never impaired; the Tathāgata does not teach the Dharma to sentient beings by means of recollecting, thinking, or deliberating. Yet, when he teaches, it is not impaired. How so? The Tathāgata’s mindfulness is never impaired in any way. Just as he is mindful and without forgetfulness, so does he teach the Dharma to sentient beings. This is the twenty-third activity of the Tathāgata. Regarding this, it is said:
U10: The mindfulness of the Teacher is never impaired; through the mindfulness of the Victor, there is nothing that remains to be recollected. When the Victor attained enlightenment and became a Buddha, he fully knew the minds of all beings without exception.
U11: Having cognized the intentions, faculties, and conduct of sentient beings, and because there is nothing further to be cognized, the Lord of Victors, abiding in spontaneously accomplished activity, teaches the Dharma to beings.
